WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.894
This is the narrated briefing for a
companion guide in the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:05.894 --> 00:00:10.725
Workbook: Defender X D R Engineering
and Operations, an implementation guide.

4
00:00:10.725 --> 00:00:13.016
One thing first: the guide is a draft,

5
00:00:13.016 --> 00:00:16.887
awaiting the editor's review,
so recheck it after the review.

6
00:00:16.887 --> 00:00:19.037
Its idea fits in a sentence.

7
00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:21.801
A Defender license is
not a defended endpoint:

8
00:00:21.801 --> 00:00:24.637
the sensor has to be on
every device and healthy,

9
00:00:24.637 --> 00:00:28.781
the prevention settings have to be
enforced and not just configured,

10
00:00:28.781 --> 00:00:32.126
and every alert has to end in
a decision someone can show.

11
00:00:32.126 --> 00:00:36.211
The guide covers Windows
clients and Windows Server only,

12
00:00:36.211 --> 00:00:41.575
for a civilian agency at fed-ramp Moderate
in Microsoft three sixty-five G C C.

13
00:00:41.575 --> 00:00:45.859
Its facts were read on Microsoft
Learn on November eleventh,

14
00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:48.687
2026, and nothing in it
was run in a tenant.

15
00:00:48.687 --> 00:00:52.550
We'll go in four parts:
why, what, who, and how.

16
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.800
Here's the goal the guide sets, as
the program's own risk-based position.

17
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.025
Every Windows device the agency
owns is onboarded and reporting.

18
00:01:05.025 --> 00:01:08.963
Its prevention settings are enforced
at the strength the design chose.

19
00:01:08.963 --> 00:01:13.875
Every Attack Surface Reduction rule is
in block, or in a recorded exception.

20
00:01:13.875 --> 00:01:17.375
Every alert lands in one
incident queue with an owner.

21
00:01:17.375 --> 00:01:20.638
And the evidence for all of it
can be produced on request.

22
00:01:20.638 --> 00:01:24.137
The guide walks each feature
through five control states.

23
00:01:24.137 --> 00:01:26.175
Designed is the decision record.

24
00:01:26.175 --> 00:01:30.763
Configured is the audit and pilot
phase, shown by policy exports.

25
00:01:30.763 --> 00:01:34.838
Enforced is block mode on the
rings, with the exclusion register.

26
00:01:34.838 --> 00:01:38.087
Evidenced is a test record
and the event it produced.

27
00:01:38.087 --> 00:01:41.725
Operated is an owner, a
cadence and drift alerts.

28
00:01:41.725 --> 00:01:46.093
A finding is ready for closure when
every applicable state has evidence;

29
00:01:46.093 --> 00:01:49.520
the authorizing official
or the assessor decides.

30
00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.808
The architecture has two trust boundaries.

31
00:01:55.808 --> 00:02:00.142
The agency's holds the devices,
the S O C's Sentinel workspace,

32
00:02:00.142 --> 00:02:02.707
automation and the evidence store.

33
00:02:02.707 --> 00:02:06.770
Microsoft's holds Defender X
D R and its control planes.

34
00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:12.624
Microsoft Learn says that for a U S
Government tenant it's built in the Azure U S

35
00:02:12.624 --> 00:02:18.295
Government environment, and G C C, G C C
High and D O D each have their own portal.

36
00:02:18.295 --> 00:02:21.295
Intune is the recommended
tool for Defender settings,

37
00:02:21.295 --> 00:02:25.762
through endpoint security policies, and
they're assigned to Entra device groups,

38
00:02:25.762 --> 00:02:29.095
because Defender management
supports device objects only.

39
00:02:29.095 --> 00:02:34.370
Automation levels, device groups and custom
detections live in the Defender portal.

40
00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.954
Telemetry flows to the tenant's instance,

41
00:02:36.954 --> 00:02:41.058
alerts become incidents, and
advanced hunting keeps thirty days.

42
00:02:41.058 --> 00:02:44.152
Servers in A W S, Google
Cloud or Oracle Cloud

43
00:02:44.152 --> 00:02:48.020
Infrastructure arrive the
way Azure Arc reaches them.

44
00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:53.640
And use one control plane per setting:
Group Policy wins over Intune by default.

45
00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.240
What does the government cloud give you?

46
00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:05.010
Microsoft Learn says Defender for
Endpoint, prevention through remediation,

47
00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.585
is available in G C C,
G C C High and D O D,

48
00:03:07.585 --> 00:03:11.402
and so is security settings
management for servers.

49
00:03:11.402 --> 00:03:13.140
Each cloud has its own portal.

50
00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:18.290
G C C reads incidents and runs hunting
through the worldwide Graph host.

51
00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:21.965
Vulnerability Management is
there, minus two features.

52
00:03:21.965 --> 00:03:26.078
Defender for Identity and Defender
for Cloud Apps are available,

53
00:03:26.078 --> 00:03:29.353
with App Governance alerts
listed as roadmap items.

54
00:03:29.353 --> 00:03:30.466
Then the gaps.

55
00:03:30.466 --> 00:03:33.085
Microsoft Security Exposure Management,

56
00:03:33.085 --> 00:03:37.353
which holds attack paths and the
exposure graph, is Public Cloud only.

57
00:03:37.353 --> 00:03:40.734
Learn doesn't say whether
Graph custom detection rules,

58
00:03:40.734 --> 00:03:45.953
automatic attack disruption, or web content
filtering on Windows reach these clouds.

59
00:03:45.953 --> 00:03:49.664
The guide's rule: not yet
specified means Learn doesn't say,

60
00:03:49.664 --> 00:03:51.165
not that it's available.

61
00:03:51.165 --> 00:03:56.080
And an unlicensed feature is a licensing
decision, not a failed control.

62
00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:05.430
Here's the path on one page: ten gates,
each with a done-when and a validate step.

63
00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:09.598
Gate one, architecture and
access: unified roles are active

64
00:04:09.598 --> 00:04:13.680
and the S O C reads incidents
through Graph in its own cloud.

65
00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.180
Two, onboarding: every in-scope device
has an active sensor, or an exception.

66
00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.368
Three, prevention: antivirus,
firewall, tamper protection,

67
00:04:23.368 --> 00:04:27.730
network protection and controlled
folder access, enforced.

68
00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:32.868
Four, Attack Surface Reduction: every
rule through audit and warn into block.

69
00:04:32.868 --> 00:04:36.955
Five, exposure: vulnerabilities
flow to an owner.

70
00:04:36.955 --> 00:04:42.343
Six, incidents: one queue, a severity
table, everything classified.

71
00:04:42.343 --> 00:04:45.743
Seven, hunting: detections
live in a repository

72
00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:48.517
and deploy through Graph after a test.

73
00:04:48.517 --> 00:04:52.405
Eight, identity and cloud-app
signals in the same incidents.

74
00:04:52.405 --> 00:04:55.480
Nine, automation set to
the response ladder.

75
00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.230
Ten, metrics and evidence every month.

76
00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:03.900
Gates one and two come first; gate ten
runs once the others have evidence.

77
00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:08.488
Now, who.

78
00:05:08.488 --> 00:05:13.183
The guide builds custom roles in Defender's
unified role-based access control,

79
00:05:13.183 --> 00:05:17.156
assigned to groups that privileged
identity management governs,

80
00:05:17.156 --> 00:05:18.962
so no one holds a standing role.

81
00:05:18.962 --> 00:05:21.561
A read role, called Security data basics,

82
00:05:21.561 --> 00:05:24.788
for analysts and the
assessor's read-only account.

83
00:05:24.788 --> 00:05:29.450
A responder role that adds alerts,
response and basic live response.

84
00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:32.807
A detection engineer role
with Detection tuning,

85
00:05:32.807 --> 00:05:36.000
manage, which the custom
detection A P I accepts.

86
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.525
Advanced live response only
for Tier zero analysts.

87
00:05:39.525 --> 00:05:44.327
Intune's Endpoint Security Manager
creates the policies, and Microsoft Learn

88
00:05:44.327 --> 00:05:49.350
says activating unified roles needs at
least Security Administrator in Entra I D.

89
00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:54.000
The RACI follows Security Operations
and Telemetry and the devices book.

90
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.333
The policy owner, an I S
S O or see-so delegate,

91
00:05:57.333 --> 00:06:01.713
is accountable for decisions,
policies and exclusions.

92
00:06:01.713 --> 00:06:04.421
The S O C owns incidents and detections,

93
00:06:04.421 --> 00:06:10.610
the help desk owns warn-mode unblock requests,
and the assessor owns the assessment.

94
00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.072
Now how, gate by gate.

95
00:06:16.072 --> 00:06:19.077
Gate one builds the roles
in the Defender portal,

96
00:06:19.077 --> 00:06:23.585
then activates unified role-based
access control workload by workload.

97
00:06:23.585 --> 00:06:26.385
Create every role and assignment first.

98
00:06:26.385 --> 00:06:30.560
Rollback is to deactivate the
workload, and the earlier roles return.

99
00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.777
Then connect the S O C's read
path in the tenant's own cloud:

100
00:06:34.777 --> 00:06:38.535
Global for G C C, U S Gov for G C
C High, U S Gov D O D for D O D.

101
00:06:38.535 --> 00:06:44.768
Microsoft Learn names Security Incident
dot Read dot All as the least-privileged

102
00:06:44.768 --> 00:06:47.060
permission to list incidents,

103
00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.460
and the signed-in user also
needs a reader or operator role.

104
00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:54.910
Threat Hunting dot Read dot
All runs hunting queries.

105
00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:57.397
Validate by listing five incidents.

106
00:06:57.397 --> 00:07:02.535
A pass is a list, or an empty one in a
new tenant, with no permission error.

107
00:07:02.535 --> 00:07:06.617
But watch the danger the guide
names: read a G C C High tenant

108
00:07:06.617 --> 00:07:11.150
from the worldwide host, and the
empty list looks like a pass.

109
00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:17.012
Gate two proves the sensor is on.

110
00:07:17.012 --> 00:07:21.125
Clients onboard through Intune's
E D R policy, ring zero, then one,

111
00:07:21.125 --> 00:07:25.633
then two and three; servers through
Defender for Servers on Azure Arc.

112
00:07:25.633 --> 00:07:30.298
Microsoft Learn says Windows Server
twenty nineteen needs a specific

113
00:07:30.298 --> 00:07:35.201
update before onboarding, so it reaches
the right government environment.

114
00:07:35.201 --> 00:07:37.963
On a pilot device, query
the sense service and

115
00:07:37.963 --> 00:07:40.800
read the onboarding state,
which should be one.

116
00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.475
Across the fleet, run the coverage
query through Graph and file the result.

117
00:07:45.475 --> 00:07:50.918
Learn lists five sensor health values:
active; inactive, typically after seven

118
00:07:50.918 --> 00:07:56.612
days without contact; no sensor data;
impaired communications; and misconfigured.

119
00:07:56.612 --> 00:07:59.988
A device that isn't active for
seven days becomes a ticket.

120
00:07:59.988 --> 00:08:02.487
A device the sensor
can't reach goes in the

121
00:08:02.487 --> 00:08:05.501
exception register with
a compensating control.

122
00:08:05.501 --> 00:08:09.445
And rollback is an offboarding
package, which expires seven days

123
00:08:09.445 --> 00:08:13.900
after download and must never go to
a device with the onboarding one.

124
00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:19.550
Gate three is prevention.

125
00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:24.647
Antivirus, firewall and tamper protection
carry the values from the devices book's

126
00:08:24.647 --> 00:08:27.749
policy library; the guide
adds network protection,

127
00:08:27.749 --> 00:08:31.738
web protection and controlled
folder access, and proves each one.

128
00:08:31.738 --> 00:08:36.384
Network protection and controlled
folder access both have an audit mode,

129
00:08:36.384 --> 00:08:38.974
value two, that logs
what they would block.

130
00:08:38.974 --> 00:08:42.688
Run them in audit on rings zero
and one for at least two weeks,

131
00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:47.537
read the audit events weekly, and add
allowed applications before enforcing.

132
00:08:47.537 --> 00:08:51.343
Antivirus, firewall and tamper
protection have no audit mode,

133
00:08:51.343 --> 00:08:54.626
so they go straight to ring
one with backout triggers.

134
00:08:54.626 --> 00:08:58.563
Then enforce ring by ring,
with exit criteria for each.

135
00:08:58.563 --> 00:09:03.593
On a device, Microsoft Learn's commands
show tamper and real-time protection,

136
00:09:03.593 --> 00:09:06.787
the modes, and the firewall
profiles as enforced.

137
00:09:06.787 --> 00:09:09.051
Rollback sets the mode back to audit.

138
00:09:09.051 --> 00:09:13.960
Learn says tamper protection is turned
off only from the console that set it.

139
00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:20.110
Gate four is Attack Surface Reduction.

140
00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:25.285
The devices book's policy names the
rules; this guide carries each stage.

141
00:09:25.285 --> 00:09:29.975
Microsoft Learn recommends putting all
rules in audit at once on ring one,

142
00:09:29.975 --> 00:09:32.897
to see which ones everyday
business triggers.

143
00:09:32.897 --> 00:09:37.773
Audit for thirty days, and decide an
exclusion or a fix for every hit.

144
00:09:37.773 --> 00:09:42.723
Then warn for fourteen days, which shows
users the block with a way to proceed.

145
00:09:42.723 --> 00:09:44.589
Warn is friction, not a bypass:

146
00:09:44.589 --> 00:09:49.490
since platform four point eighteen point
two six oh six oh, unblocking needs

147
00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:54.547
administrator approval, and a persistent
need belongs in a per-rule exclusion.

148
00:09:54.547 --> 00:09:59.029
Two rules don't support warn, the
credential-stealing rule for the local security

149
00:09:59.029 --> 00:10:01.570
authority and the Office
code-injection rule,

150
00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:03.710
so they go from audit straight to block.

151
00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:08.985
Rollback moves a rule one stage back, but
Microsoft says an exclusion is better.

152
00:10:08.985 --> 00:10:12.413
On servers, Learn lists a
known issue where a rule shows

153
00:10:12.413 --> 00:10:16.660
compliant without being enforced,
so check the device itself.

154
00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:25.069
Gate five covers what Defender adds to
the vulnerability program that Security

155
00:10:25.069 --> 00:10:29.172
Operations and Telemetry runs, and what
it can't do in a government cloud.

156
00:10:29.172 --> 00:10:34.160
Microsoft Learn says Security Exposure
Management, which holds attack paths,

157
00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:37.383
the exposure graph and
critical asset management,

158
00:10:37.383 --> 00:10:41.297
is available in Public Cloud only,
not in U S Government clouds.

159
00:10:41.297 --> 00:10:44.285
So a Zero Trust Assessment
test that needs it is

160
00:10:44.285 --> 00:10:47.348
recorded as not applicable
in the tenant's cloud.

161
00:10:47.348 --> 00:10:49.761
That's a disposition, not a failure.

162
00:10:49.761 --> 00:10:54.285
What the government clouds do have is
Vulnerability Management, core and premium,

163
00:10:54.285 --> 00:10:58.410
without two features, and Secure Score
with some recommendations missing.

164
00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:00.520
The interim path for attack paths:

165
00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:05.872
prioritize by exposure level and internet-facing
devices from the device info table,

166
00:11:05.872 --> 00:11:08.585
and use the Tier zero
inventory from Access

167
00:11:08.585 --> 00:11:11.148
and Privilege as the critical-asset list.

168
00:11:11.148 --> 00:11:14.018
A vulnerability without
an owner is a finding

169
00:11:14.018 --> 00:11:16.510
against the inventory control, C M eight.

170
00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:20.010
And Sentinel can't query
the vulnerability tables.

171
00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.385
Gate six is incidents.

172
00:11:25.385 --> 00:11:29.161
Microsoft Learn says Defender
X D R correlates alerts that

173
00:11:29.161 --> 00:11:32.860
share attack techniques or an
attacker into one incident.

174
00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:38.140
The response ladder and device
isolation belong to Security Operations

175
00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:43.073
and Telemetry; this gate fixes
severity, triage, evidence and hand-off.

176
00:11:43.073 --> 00:11:45.723
The severity table is
the program's design.

177
00:11:45.723 --> 00:11:50.201
A high incident is triaged within an
hour and contained on a gated rung,

178
00:11:50.201 --> 00:11:53.984
where the playbook prepares
and a named approver releases,

179
00:11:53.984 --> 00:11:58.772
with the incident response lead and the
I S S O deciding on U S cert reporting.

180
00:11:58.772 --> 00:12:02.772
A medium one within twenty-four
hours, enriched and ticketed.

181
00:12:02.772 --> 00:12:08.397
A low one within seventy-two hours,
observed, with a tuning review if it recurs.

182
00:12:08.397 --> 00:12:11.620
Servers and privileged or
emergency accounts are

183
00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:14.536
never contained by a
playbook; a human decides.

184
00:12:14.536 --> 00:12:19.272
To validate, export the open incidents
and one incident with its alerts.

185
00:12:19.272 --> 00:12:21.297
Every open incident has an owner,

186
00:12:21.297 --> 00:12:25.790
and every closed one carries a
classification and determination.

187
00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:34.749
Gate seven puts detections in a repository
and deploys them through the Graph A P I,

188
00:12:34.749 --> 00:12:39.115
following the rule lifecycle in
Security Operations and Telemetry.

189
00:12:39.115 --> 00:12:42.942
Each rule file starts disabled,
and leaves out automated actions,

190
00:12:42.942 --> 00:12:45.352
because containment stays on the ladder.

191
00:12:45.352 --> 00:12:49.800
Every rule has a validation dataset:
a known-bad event it must find,

192
00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.765
and a known-good window
it must stay quiet in.

193
00:12:52.765 --> 00:12:55.615
A rule that can't be shown
to fire isn't finished.

194
00:12:55.615 --> 00:13:00.397
Test the query against the last thirty
days, which is all advanced hunting keeps,

195
00:13:00.397 --> 00:13:03.440
then post the file to the
detection rules endpoint.

196
00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:08.515
Enabling is a change to the status in
the repository, reviewed and redeployed.

197
00:13:08.515 --> 00:13:14.280
Microsoft Learn's hunting A P I is available
in U S Government L four and L five,

198
00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:18.490
but the detection rules A P I
has no national cloud table,

199
00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:23.615
so in G C C High and D O D, create the
rule in the portal from the same file.

200
00:13:23.615 --> 00:13:28.090
Pass: the deployed rules
match the repository, exactly.

201
00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:33.152
Gates eight and nine.

202
00:13:33.152 --> 00:13:36.382
Gate eight puts identity
and S A A S behavior

203
00:13:36.382 --> 00:13:39.341
into the same incidents as the endpoint.

204
00:13:39.341 --> 00:13:43.830
Defender for Identity's sensors go
on domain controllers, A D F S,

205
00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:49.528
A D C S and Entra Connect servers, starting
with one domain controller per domain.

206
00:13:49.528 --> 00:13:53.240
Microsoft Learn gives each
cloud its own sensor endpoint.

207
00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:56.704
Two gaps: App Governance
alerts are roadmap items,

208
00:13:56.704 --> 00:13:59.323
and Cloud Apps behaviors aren't in G C C.

209
00:13:59.323 --> 00:14:04.815
Validate with a correlation query: users
with alerts from more than one source.

210
00:14:04.815 --> 00:14:08.765
Gate nine sets Defender's automation
to match the response ladder.

211
00:14:08.765 --> 00:14:12.445
Pilot ring one's device group
at the semi level that requires

212
00:14:12.445 --> 00:14:16.052
approval for all folders, and
review two weeks of approvals.

213
00:14:16.052 --> 00:14:20.567
Then workstations go to full, which
Learn recommends, and servers stay

214
00:14:20.567 --> 00:14:25.315
at a semi level, because the program
never isolates a server autonomously.

215
00:14:25.315 --> 00:14:28.395
Exclude the emergency
access accounts and the S

216
00:14:28.395 --> 00:14:31.552
O C's own service accounts
from attack disruption.

217
00:14:31.552 --> 00:14:34.440
Every disruption action can be undone.

218
00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:39.865
Gate ten makes it operate.

219
00:14:39.865 --> 00:14:43.698
The guide's metrics measure
protected coverage and effectiveness,

220
00:14:43.698 --> 00:14:45.340
not whether a setting exists.

221
00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:50.134
Protected coverage is devices with an
active sensor over the in-scope devices in

222
00:14:50.134 --> 00:14:54.928
the inventory, because a policy says
nothing about devices that never checked in.

223
00:14:54.928 --> 00:14:58.478
Enforcement depth counts devices
with every Attack Surface

224
00:14:58.478 --> 00:15:02.028
Reduction rule in block, because
audit mode protects nothing.

225
00:15:02.028 --> 00:15:07.865
Mean time to triage and contain, by severity:
detection that waits is a warehouse.

226
00:15:07.865 --> 00:15:12.328
Detection validity: a rule that can't
be shown to fire isn't a control.

227
00:15:12.328 --> 00:15:17.932
And synthetic-event success: the detection
test, the network protection test site,

228
00:15:17.932 --> 00:15:23.453
and each rule's demonstration, run on ring
zero, each producing its expected event.

229
00:15:23.453 --> 00:15:28.175
The cadence is weekly for sensor health
and drift, quarterly for tuning,

230
00:15:28.175 --> 00:15:33.139
exceptions and an exercise, and monthly
for the evidence run, which runs the

231
00:15:33.139 --> 00:15:38.460
read-only queries, exports the incidents
and hashes every file for the I S S O.

232
00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:44.073
To close, the dangers.

233
00:15:44.073 --> 00:15:46.646
A rule assigned to user
groups never applies,

234
00:15:46.646 --> 00:15:50.123
because Defender management
supports device objects only.

235
00:15:50.123 --> 00:15:55.685
Intune and Group Policy both setting a
rule means Group Policy silently wins.

236
00:15:55.685 --> 00:15:58.392
A server's compliance
report can show a rule

237
00:15:58.392 --> 00:16:00.948
enforced when it isn't,
so check the server.

238
00:16:00.948 --> 00:16:04.917
Exposure Management assumed in a
government cloud stalls the work,

239
00:16:04.917 --> 00:16:07.398
or makes an assessment
test read as failed.

240
00:16:07.398 --> 00:16:10.069
Attack disruption
containing a server or an

241
00:16:10.069 --> 00:16:12.885
emergency account means
an outage or a lockout.

242
00:16:12.885 --> 00:16:16.217
And hunting evidence older
than thirty days isn't there,

243
00:16:16.217 --> 00:16:17.810
so the monthly run files it.

244
00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:22.194
The guide is plain about what it couldn't
verify: the detection rules A P I,

245
00:16:22.194 --> 00:16:26.355
attack disruption and web content
filtering in government clouds,

246
00:16:26.355 --> 00:16:31.185
and the detection test on Windows ten and
eleven, which Learn's page doesn't list.

247
00:16:31.185 --> 00:16:32.885
Nothing was run in a tenant.

248
00:16:32.885 --> 00:16:35.893
Next, read Security
Operations and Telemetry

249
00:16:35.893 --> 00:16:38.585
and the devices book,
which own the design.

250
00:16:38.585 --> 00:16:39.400
Thank you.
